  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Thursday) \nIssued at 1201 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA slightly drier airmass will remain in place on Saturday. The \npressure gradient will increase Friday night into Saturday as an \narea of surface low pressure develops and moves into the \nPlains/Midwest region, ahead of a slow-moving cold front. \nSouth/southwest winds will increase by late Saturday morning and \ninto the afternoon. Periodic gusts 25 to 30 mph will be possible,\nspecifically across northeast OK. This will create limited fire \nweather concerns across much of the area as minimum afternoon \nrelative humidity values drop between 20 to 30 percent. Luckily, \nthe highest wind speeds will remain across northeast OK and the \nlowest humidity values will occur across southeast OK/western AR \nand will not be collocated. However, given the hot temperatures,\nincreased wind speeds, and dry airmass in place, wildfire safety \nprecautions should be taken on Saturday.\n\nMid/upper-level ridging will begin to flatten Saturday \nevening/night as a shortwave trough moves over the Plains and a \nsurface cold front progresses southward across the Plains. The \nslow-moving front eventually moves into northeast OK late Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. There will be a small window of limited\nsevere potential near and just ahead of the frontal boundary late\nSaturday night into Sunday morning. The highest severe threat \nwill remain near the KS/OK border, with instability dropping off \nsignificantly south of the KS/OK border. Damaging wind gusts and\nlocally heavy rainfall will be the only concerns with storms. \nLatest NWP models show the front will slow down as it pushes \nacross the southern half of our area on Monday. This will keep \nsoutheast OK and western AR unseasonably warm through at least \nMonday, with afternoon temperatures rising into the low to mid \n90s. There is still some uncertainty how far south the cooler air \nwill reach, even beyond Monday. Behind the cold front, \ntemperatures will be much cooler, closer to or just slightly above\naverage, with daytime highs in the mid 80s through the first half\nof the upcoming workweek. The front will bring a decent chance of\nrain/storms Sunday night and through Monday, with pockets of\nmoderate to heavy rainfall, but general amounts should stay below\nan inch for most locations. Low shower and thunderstorm chances \nwill persist mid to late week, despite upper-level ridging trying\nto build back in. \n\nMejia",
